※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=42 diag=none

※ An array written where nothing says what type is wanted of it takes its type
※ from its elements: they are of one type and there are as many of them as are
※ written, which is the whole of an array's type.  Nothing else could say it,
※ and refusing to read it would refuse a thing every part of which is written
※ down.

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    ※ looked in where it stands, with no name and no type anywhere
    let one: u8 = ⟦42u8, 2u8⟧⟦0⟧
    ※ the same, a dimension deep
    let two: u8 = ⟦⟦1u8, 2u8⟧, ⟦3u8, 4u8⟧⟧⟦1, 1⟧
    one - two + 4u8
